## 1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout` (src/petty.ts:10-19)

**Evidence:** *"6 distinct property reads: currency, plan, region, retry, role, status"*

The bag is typed (`CheckoutOptions`), so the detector isn't complaining about `any`. It's complaining that one parameter carries six unrelated concerns — and five of those six fields are `string`. Nothing distinguishes a `plan` from a `role` from a `region` at the type level.

**What an agent gets wrong today:**

- **Swapped fields type-check perfectly.** `describeCheckout({ plan: role, role: plan, ... })` compiles clean and produces a plausible-looking key. There is no test to catch it.
- **The join order is an undeclared wire format.** `[plan, role, region, currency, status, retry].join(":")` is a cache-key/log-line format that exists only as positional array order in a function body. An agent told "add `locale` to the checkout descriptor" adds a field and appends it to the array — silently changing the key for every existing consumer. Nothing in the file says that string is durable.
- **The name lies about optionality.** `options` reads as "optional"; the interface says all six are required.

**Refactor** — narrow the types so the fields stop being interchangeable, and name the format:

```ts
type Plan = "free" | "pro" | "enterprise";
type Role = "owner" | "admin" | "member";
type Region = "us" | "eu" | "apac";
type Currency = "usd" | "eur" | "gbp";
type CheckoutStatus = "pending" | "paid" | "failed";

export interface CheckoutDescriptor {
  plan: Plan;
  role: Role;
  region: Region;
  currency: Currency;
  status: CheckoutStatus;
  retry: boolean;
}

/** Field order is the serialized key format — appending changes every existing key. */
const CHECKOUT_KEY_FIELDS = [
  "plan", "role", "region", "currency", "status", "retry",
] as const satisfies readonly (keyof CheckoutDescriptor)[];

export function describeCheckout(descriptor: CheckoutDescriptor): string {
  return CHECKOUT_KEY_FIELDS.map((field) => String(descriptor[field])).join(":");
}
```

Unions kill the swap bug for four of the six fields at zero runtime cost. Lifting the order into a named `const` with the comment turns "append a field" from an invisible edit into a deliberate one.

The detector's own `fix_shape` goes further — *"split the options bag into named, narrowly-typed records"*. That's the follow-on if this grows: `describeCheckout(subject, locale, attempt)` where `subject` is `{plan, role}`, `locale` is `{region, currency}`, `attempt` is `{status, retry}`. I'd hold off until there's a second caller — with zero callers today, the union narrowing buys most of the safety for a fraction of the churn.

